Introduction
A single-symbol Expert Advisor can afford to hardcode assumptions about its instrument. It knows the pip value, the contract size, the minimum lot, and the tick size because it was written and tested against one specific symbol. When that EA is deployed inside a multi-currency portfolio framework, those assumptions break immediately.
Consider a portfolio holding EURUSD, XAUUSD (spot gold), US30 (a CFD index), and USDJPY simultaneously. The contract size on EURUSD is 100,000 units of base currency. On XAUUSD, it is 100 troy ounces. On US30, it may be one unit of index value. On USDJPY, the pip value in account currency depends on the current USDJPY exchange rate. A position-sizing formula that applies a fixed dollar-risk-per-lot calculation uniformly across these symbols will produce badly wrong sizes for most of them.
The solution is a metadata layer that queries the broker's contract specifications at runtime, normalizes tick values into a common account-currency denominator, and stores the results in a structure accessible to every component of the portfolio framework. This layer makes no trading decisions. It exists solely to ensure that every calculation consuming position size, risk budget, or monetary value is working with accurate, symbol-specific figures.
Note: If you missed Part 1 of this series, you can read it
here
.
What the Broker Provides and What Must Be Derived
MetaTrader 5 exposes contract specification data through SymbolInfoDouble(), SymbolInfoInteger(), and SymbolInfoString(). The raw values returned by these functions are not always directly usable. Several key quantities must be derived rather than read directly.
Tick value in account currency is the most critical derived quantity. MetaTrader 5 provides SYMBOL_TRADE_TICK_VALUE, which returns the monetary value of one tick move already expressed in account (deposit) currency. When the symbol's profit currency differs from the account currency — EURJPY on a USD account, for example — the terminal performs the cross-rate conversion internally before returning this value; the registry does not perform any conversion of its own, it only reads the result. Because the underlying cross rate moves, the value this call returns is only correct at the moment it is read, so the metadata layer must refresh it periodically rather than reading it once at startup.
Lot size normalization: requires the minimum lot (SYMBOL_VOLUME_MIN), maximum lot (SYMBOL_VOLUME_MAX), and lot step (SYMBOL_VOLUME_STEP). A computed lot size must be rounded down to the nearest lot step and capped at the maximum before submission. If the resulting size falls below the broker's minimum lot, this implementation returns 0.0 rather than rounding up to the minimum, so it does not silently exceed the caller's intended risk. Skipping this produces an ERR_INVALID_VOLUME rejection at the broker level.
Digits and point size: determine how price levels are formatted for stop-loss and take-profit calculations. SYMBOL_DIGITS gives the number of decimal places, and SYMBOL_POINT gives the value of one point. For five-digit brokers, one pip equals ten points on most FX pairs. The metadata layer stores both values and provides a helper that converts a pip distance into the correct point count for a given symbol.
Figure 1: Contract Specification Comparison: EURUSD and XAUUSD read directly since their profit currency already matches the account. USDJPY, US30, and USDCAD need a cross-rate or tick/point ratio derivation to get an accurate account-currency value.
Dynamic Symbol Loading
Rather than hard-coding the managed symbol list inside the Portfolio Controller, the framework in this part reads a plain-text universe file — one symbol per line — from the terminal's shared Common\Files folder. The Portfolio Controller reads this file at initialization, validates each entry against the terminal's market watch, and builds its internal universe at runtime.
This has practical advantages. Adding or removing symbols from the portfolio does not require recompiling any EA. The list can be edited while the terminal is running, since it is re-read on every controller initialization. Symbols unavailable in market watch are skipped with a logged warning rather than causing a hard failure.
The universe file must be saved as plain single-byte (ANSI) text, for example, Windows-1252 on Western European systems, not as UTF-8 or UTF-16. Many text editors default to a UTF-16 "Unicode" encoding when saving .txt files, which stores every character as two bytes rather than one, and the registry opens the file with the FILE_ANSI flag, which reads single-byte text only. If the file is UTF-16, or contains multi-byte UTF-8 sequences, it will be misread: byte pairs or byte sequences become garbled characters, and extracted symbol names fail validation. Since symbol names are ASCII in practice, saving as plain single-byte text from the start avoids this entirely.
Validation against market watch is mandatory before loading metadata. SymbolInfoDouble() called on a symbol not present in market watch returns 0.0 without setting an error code, which would otherwise produce silently incorrect metadata. SymbolSelect() adds a symbol to market watch if it is available on the broker's server, after which the metadata query can proceed safely.
Figure 2: Dynamic Symbol Loading Sequence: each symbol is validated with SymbolSelect() and its trade mode before contract specifications are queried and stored. Invalid symbols are skipped with a logged warning and the loop continues to the next entry.
The Symbol Metadata Structure
All contract specification data for one symbol is encapsulated in a single structure. Centralizing this data prevents the scattered SymbolInfoDouble() calls that proliferate in unstructured code and eliminates the risk of inconsistent values being used in different parts of the system.
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
//|                                              SymbolMetadata.mqh  |
//|         Contract specification structure and metadata utilities  |
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
#ifndef
SYMBOL_METADATA_MQH
#define
SYMBOL_METADATA_MQH
//--- Symbol metadata container
struct
SymbolMetadata
  {
string
Symbol
;
// Symbol name as returned by the terminal
double
ContractSize;
// Units per standard lot
double
TickSize;
// Minimum price movement
double
TickValue;
// Account-currency value of one tick per lot
double
PointSize;
// Value of SYMBOL_POINT
int
Digits
;
// Decimal places in price
double
VolumeMin;
// Minimum lot size
double
VolumeMax;
// Maximum lot size
double
VolumeStep;
// Lot increment step
double
SpreadPoints;
// Current spread in points
string
ProfitCurrency;
// Currency in which profits are denominated (also the quote currency for a standard FX pair)
string
BaseCurrency;
// Base currency (first in pair)
bool
IsLoaded;
// True if metadata was loaded successfully
datetime
LoadTimestamp;
// Time at which metadata was last refreshed
};
#endif
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
The IsLoaded flag and LoadTimestamp field serve distinct purposes. IsLoaded indicates whether the initial metadata load succeeded. LoadTimestamp supports the periodic refresh mechanism: any consumer of the metadata can check whether the tick value is stale and request a refresh if the timestamp is older than a configurable threshold.
The Symbol Registry Class
The registry class owns the metadata array, manages loading and refresh operations, and provides the access interface used by the Portfolio Controller and every Instrument Agent. No other component calls SymbolInfoDouble() directly after initialization. All symbol data flows through the registry.
MQL5 permits reference parameters only in function argument lists, not as local variable aliases. Every method below reads and writes struct fields through direct array indexing — m_Symbols[idx].Field — rather than through a local alias, since MQL5 has no equivalent to a C++-style local reference binding.
