Tick Value Normalization Across Asset Classes
The PointsToAccountCurrency() method deserves closer examination, because the derivation it performs is one of the most consistently misunderstood calculations in MQL5 development.
MetaTrader 5 reports SYMBOL_TRADE_TICK_VALUE as the profit or loss in account currency generated by one tick move on one lot. A tick move equals SYMBOL_TRADE_TICK_SIZE in price terms. The point size reported by SYMBOL_POINT may or may not equal the tick size — on most FX pairs they are equal, but on CFD symbols such as indices or commodities, the tick size is often a multiple of the point size.
To convert a distance measured in points into an account-currency monetary value, the formula is:
MonetaryValue = Points × (PointSize / TickSize) × TickValue × Lots
The ratio PointSize / TickSize adjusts for the case where the symbol's minimum price increment (tick size) is larger than one point. For standard FX pairs where tick size equals point size, this ratio is 1.0 and the formula reduces to the familiar Points × TickValue × Lots. For an index CFD where tick size might be 1.0 and point size 0.01, the ratio is 0.01, correctly scaling down the monetary value per point.
