Updating the Instrument Agent to Use Metadata-Derived Lot Limits
The Instrument Agent requires two changes relative to the framework skeleton established earlier in this series. First, its lot size calculation now reads from the per-symbol lot limit global published by the Portfolio Controller, rather than a single uniform lot ceiling. Second, it gains a local CSymbolRegistry instance scoped to its own symbol, used for lot-size normalization and pip-to-point conversion when computing position size. Stop-loss and take-profit placement are not implemented in this part — the order calls below submit both at zero — since the signal logic that would size a stop is still a stub; both are introduced once the risk model built in later parts has a distance to size them from.
The agent checks readiness on every tick until the controller is confirmed. After a timeout, it continues checking on the timer even in degraded mode. This matters because controller startup time is not fixed — a large symbol universe or a slow first contact with the broker can push initialization past an agent's timeout window. Without repeated checks, an agent may remain in degraded mode after the controller becomes available. In that case it would keep trading on fallback defaults instead of the controller-published limits. Re-checking on every timer cycle lets the agent recover automatically the moment the controller's readiness flag appears. Recovery here means the agent stops sizing new trades from fallback values once the controller is confirmed ready — it does not retroactively adjust or flag any position already opened under degraded-mode sizing before that point.
